<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Latium</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Latium was the region of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiber-river">Tiber</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-naples">Bay of Naples</a>, inhabited by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latins</a>, who gave their name to the Latin language. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> rose to dominate the Latin League and absorbed the region after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-war">Latin War</a> of 340–338 BCE. Its name survives in the modern Italian region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lazio">Lazio</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/latium.jpg" alt="Latium" width="320" height="227" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: William Robert Shepherd · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Latium_et_Campania.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
